### README ###
## NOTE: this file describes the original command-line toolbox. For installing and using
## the current MCS (web page, keys, backups, off-box copy) read INSTALL.md in the MCS folder.
## Sat 31 Aug 2024 04:11:32 AM EDT
## Author: snkiz
## License: Creative Commons. 
## It's just some bash I cobbled together from bits of stack exchange.

## The latest Official Mincraft server jar can be found here;
## *** https://www.minecraft.net/en-us/download/server ***

This packege is a number of scripts to help you set up a basic secure method of 
running and managing multipule minecraft servers with nothing more than systemd.
Be they vanilla or modded, you supply the jar. Some things you will run as your
Linux user and some things are run as the minecraft Linux user. Things in 
<these brakets> are varibles, do not include the brackets. 
**NOTES** Are very IMPORTANT! Read them.
Some of this is run with SUDO. Do Not just trust me and run it. Look it over,
it all has comments. This is the longest document in the buch.

These comands are all run as your user. ( <yourusername>@<hostname> )
  - MCSuser
  - systemctl <start/stop/staus> MCS@<instance>
  - systemctl <enable/disable> MCS@<instance>   ## Auto start. Run as sudo.
  - journalctl -u MCS@<instance> -f              ## Minecraft console log
  - echo "<comand>" > /run/MCS@<instance>.stdin  ## Send the console commands
  
./MCSuser 
  Creates a system user and group called minecraft. Creates a directory
  owned by minecraft where your server files live and adds some tools. 
  Installs a systemd service to control your servers. 
  It only does those if nessary or requested. So it safe to run on an existing 
  install. It always sets file permissions. So you can re-run this if you have
  issues with those.
 
To run comands as the minecraft user you will need to run:
  - xhost +si:localuser:minecraft     ## **NOTES** To give minecraft user GUI access.
  - sudo -iu minecraft ## log in as the minecraft user.
  
  
Once logged in ( you will see your prompt change from <yourusername>@<hostname> 
to minecraft@<hostname>. ) Then you can change directory ( ls /opt/mcs/bin )
and run any of the MCS<tools>. **NOTES** Except MCSuser.

./MCScreate
  checks that you have agreed to mojang's eula and asks you to name your server.
  Then creates a new instance folder, and copies some files. It then runs the other
  tools in the following order. **NOTES** If you are not running an xserver,
  Browse and Import will not work properly. You will have to copy your jar 
  manually, and run MCSconf to at least fill in the feilds marked NULL.

./MCSimport
  Opens the import directory in a file browser so you may copy your jar file and
  any mods and configs you may have. It then copies them to the instance and cleans
  the import directory.
  
./MCSconf
  Copies the service.conf and server.properties if they don't exist and prompts
  you to edit those files. The service.conf is the configuration for the 
  systemd service. server.properties is the main minecraft configuation file.
  **NOTES** Edit this before the server is run for the first time if you want a
  custom seed or experimental features.
  
./MCSbrowse
  Just opens the file browser either in the the <instance> folder or the minecraft
  home folder. **NOTES** If using wayland ( pkexec -u minecraft <filebrowser> ) 
  should be used instead. Your milage may vary.
  
./MCShelp
  A more concise version of this file.
  
###############################################################################
 
Special note:
 A kind of hacky workaround to managing your mods is to use prism to do it for you.
 https://flathub.org/apps/org.prismlauncher.PrismLauncher
 Prism has dependency checking, and with this method you can test the mod load out
 in a local prism client to make sure it all works. Prism has rudimenty server 
 support, but this won't be using that. 
 *Be aware prism is meant for you to run a client, this is not supported in
 any way. Some mods are not compible with servers. You must read the
 discriptions! And ensure your mods are meant for servers.

 So, what you do is use flatseal https://flathub.org/apps/com.github.tchx84.Flatseal
 to give prism access to your instance folder. Then create a prism instance 
 with the matching version ( mcversion and jar, vanilla, fabic, modpak, etc. )
 Now click the folder button for that instance. If you haven't put any mods on
 your server yet, copy the mod and config folders to the server instance, then
 delete them from the prism folder.
 Open a terminal in that folder and use:
 - ln -dsv /opt/minecraft/instance/<instance>/config config
 - ln -dsv /opt/minecraft/instance/<instance>/mods mods
 Do the same for any file or folder that a mod drops in the root minecraft
 folder. 
 Last you restart prism. Go to your instance and click the edit button, click
 the mods button and update.
 
 You are good to go. Just keep in mind prism can not update your server jar.
 And again, this is a hack.

